1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The ancient Arabs saw — a
desert zoo.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Around the rise of Islam, Bedouin star
names lived in poems. Later scholars
wrote them down.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Of 100-plus constellations, many are
animals: gazelles, oryx, wolves, hyenas,
camels, wild donkeys, eagles, ravens…

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
It sprawls from Gemini all the way to
Virgo.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Legend says its tail struck the ground,
and the gazelles leapt in fright.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Three star pairs below the Great Bear:
the hoofprints. Alula, Tania, Talitha —
first, second, third leap.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Fomalhaut and Diphda are two male
ostriches, al-Ẓalīmān. The faint stars
between them are the chicks.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Cetus holds a few mother ostriches.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Next door, in Eridanus: a nest, with
eggs and even eggshells.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
One star’s official name, Azha, comes
from the Arabic for “ostrich nest” —
miscopied.

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
In Sagittarius, four ostriches walk down
to the Milky Way river to drink. Four
more head back.

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Arabian ostriches died out in the 20th
century. In the sky, they remain.

13
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Lunar New Year: the Vermilion Bird.

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

